Admission Controllers  - 

kubectl. -   kubeapi server. -  Create pod 

When the request to create a pod is generated , it goes through an authentication process which is done through certificate mentioned in file  [ /root/.kube/config ] . It check whether the user is valid or not

once authentication is completed, it goes through authorization process , We check whether the user has permission to perform the request access - which is done through RBAC 

With RBAC - We can control access for create pods / delete pods . RBAC  controls things at the api level. IT doesn't go beyond 

Some actions as mentioned below cannot be managed using RBAC , That’s where Admission controllers come into play 
Only permit from certain registry 
We don’t want to use latest tag 
we don’t want pod to run as root user 
metadata should contain labels 

Admission Controllers help us implement better security measures to manage k8s cluster 

Number of Admission Controllers - 
AlwaysPullImages - Ensures that everytime a pod is created, image is pulled 
DefaultStorageClass - This admission controller observes creation of PersistentVolumeClaim objects that do not request any specific storage class and automatically adds a default storage class to them.
EventRateLimit - This admission controller mitigates the problem where the API server gets flooded by requests to store new Events. Can set up a limit to api server so that api server doesn't get flooded with requests 

NamespaceExists -  If the namespace referenced from a request doesn't exist, the request is rejected.

NamespaceAutoProvision - If we try to create pod in a namespace that doesn't exist, if NamespaceAutoProvision  admission controller is enabled, it will auto create the namespace and schedule the pod 

View Enabled Admission Controllers - 
kube-apiserver -h | grep enable-admission-plugins

For kubeadm setup - 
# kubectl exec kube-apiserver-controlplane -n kube-system -- kube-apiserver -h | grep enable-admission-plugins

# ps -ef | grep kube-apiserver | grep admission-plugins

Enable Admission Controllers - 
kube-apiserver.service file-
--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestrictions,NamespaceAutoprovision
--disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass

in kubeadm setup - 
/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.service
- --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestrictions,NamespaceAutoprovision

kube-apiserver --enable-admission-plugins=NamespaceLifecycle,LimitRanger ..
kube-apiserver --disable-admission-plugins=PodNodeSelector,AlwaysDeny ...

Note that the NamespaceExists and NamespaceAutoProvision admission controllers are deprecated and now replaced by NamespaceLifecycle admission controller.
The NamespaceLifecycle admission controller will make sure that requests
to a non-existent namespace is rejected and that the default namespaces such as
default, kube-system and kube-public cannot be deleted.

Validating Admission Controller - NamespaceExists,  They are those, that validate the request & allow / deny 
Mutating Admission Controller - DefaultStorageClass ,  They are those, that change the request . When we create a pvc with no storageclass assigned, by default it gets assigned to default storageclass

There can be admission controllers that can do both validate and mutate. 


Note- Generally mutating Admission Controller  are invoked first followed by Validating Admission Controller . This is so cause any change made by thr mutating controller can later be validated by Validating Admission Controller 

To support Extenal mutating controllers, we have MutatingAdmissionWebhook  &  ValidatingAdmissionWebhook 


